+\subsection{Independent inferential audit and bounded sensitivities}
+\label{sec:audit-sensitivity}
+The M2 audit independently reconstructs all 198 saved cross-source tau
+intervals across 11 metrics, including the 18 primary intervals, and all
+72 intervals underlying the 36 primary saved-supported reversals. It also
+reconstructs 6,890 cross-metric difference intervals and six rank intervals
+covering ANWG versus mean latency at LOW and HIGH\_PRESSURE in all sources.
+The independent script imports no production analysis routines. All
+checked endpoints and tail fractions match to numerical tolerance.
+
+Reproduction requires more than a nominal seed. The primary pipeline uses
+one NumPy random stream initialized at zero, consumed first by all 198
+ranking comparisons and then by reversal comparisons reaching the interval
+stage. Its row order and preceding draws matter. The extension derives
+separate CRC32 seeds from condition, metric and policy keys. M2 records
+input hashes, ordering, seeds, algorithms and software versions. These
+facts establish computational repeatability, not empirical coverage.
+
+\paragraph{The 66 Friedman records.}
+For each metric/region, the blocks are all 120 windows, pooled across
+sources, and treatments are policies. The complete-policy rule includes
+all 11 policies for the three always-defined metrics (18 records), but
+excludes the historical vLLM policy for each conditional metric (48
+records), leaving 10 treatments. It does not drop those policies only
+within the incomplete source. Independent average-rank sums with tie
+correction reproduce every statistic and its chi-square tail probability;
+the audit supplies each exact input matrix.
+
+This omnibus tests a policy main effect in the pooled block design under
+its null assumptions. It is not a source-by-policy interaction test and
+cannot establish cross-source ranking heterogeneity. The pipeline computes
+it first but does not condition pairwise analysis on its rejection. The
+pairwise reversal claim thus receives no additional validation from a
+small omnibus $p$-value. Independence of blocks and the suitability of the
+asymptotic null approximation remain assumptions, even with 120 nominal
+blocks. Likewise, saved Kendall/Spearman analytic $p$-values concern
+association across the selected policy coordinates, not uncertainty from
+sampling workload windows; we do not use them as window-level evidence.
+
+\paragraph{Multiplicity and finite resolution.}
+Thirty of 36 primary intersection--union tail quantities are zero in
+2,000 draws. A zero means no relevant tail draw occurred, not zero error
+probability. As an arithmetic sensitivity, M2 retains the complete family
+size, assigning untested records a placeholder of one. This is not a new
+calibrated test. The number of saved-supported records also passing BH
+changes from 36 to 30 for the primary analysis, and from 3,207 to 3,006 for
+the cross-metric extension.
+
+Adding one to each tail count and dividing by 2,001 before doubling gives
+minimum two-sided value $2/2001$. With full families, the corresponding
+counts are 30 and 2,971. Applying the harmonic-factor dependence adjustment
+of Benjamini--Yekutieli to those same quantities gives zero and 1,940.
+These different counts expose sensitivity to the testing interpretation;
+none is endorsed as an FDR-controlled discovery count. Valid null
+$p$-values are required even for a dependence adjustment. In particular,
+a Monte Carlo continuity adjustment cannot turn the uncentered empirical
+bootstrap into a proven null test.
+
+\paragraph{Dependence stress test.}
+A separate post-hoc calculation orders the 40 selected windows of each
+source by valid-row position and groups consecutive sets of five. It
+resamples eight groups with replacement for 2,000 draws, preserving policy
+pairing within each group. Thirty of the 36 primary saved-supported
+records retain zero-excluding intervals on both sides. This is a bounded
+stress test, not an estimated optimal block length or validated 95\%
+procedure: selected windows are irregularly separated, and eight groups
+provide limited information. No provider-level bootstrap is justified by
+three sources spanning only two infrastructure lineages.
+
+Together these checks support retaining the observed ordering changes and
+historical procedural labels while withdrawing their claimed error-rate
+interpretation. They do not show that the six sensitivity losses are false
+reversals, or that the remaining 30 are certain. More resampling of the
+same windows cannot create independent workload coverage, rectify policy
+comparability, or settle faithful native transfer.
